<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  macOS only lets an app hear a microphone or see through a camera if the bundle
  says why it wants one. Tauri merges this file into the bundle's generated
  Info.plist at build time, so it holds only what the generator does not already
  write.

  Microphone: without this key the system does not show the permission dialog
  and does not list the app under Privacy & Security > Microphone: it silently
  refuses, and cpal hands back a stream of silence. That is the audio-input bug
  on macOS release builds (#59). `tauri dev` never showed it, because a dev run
  borrows the permission of the terminal or editor that launched it. The webview
  never opens a microphone; the sound of a take is the engine's, through cpal,
  and it is cpal that needs this key.

  Camera: without this key `getUserMedia` does not prompt and does not fail with
  a message. The app is killed, which is the worst of the three outcomes and the
  one nobody can debug from a bug report.

  Each sentence is the one a macOS dialog puts after "Yames would like to access
  the ...": what it is for, and where it goes. `plans/WEBSITE_DECISIONS.md`: the
  reader is a musician, never a developer.

  The matching entitlements, `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` and
  `com.apple.security.device.camera`, live in `entitlements.plist`. A signed,
  notarized build runs with the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime
  refuses both without them, whatever this file says. Both files are needed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Yames listens to your instrument to score your timing and coach your practice. The sound is analysed on this Mac and is never uploaded.</string>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Yames records you playing, so you can watch it back beside the notes. The recording is kept on this Mac and is never uploaded.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
